  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.2. Increased capacity of Member States to formulate and implement policies and strategies for creating decent work in the rural economy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">To support employment policy-making processes at national and local government levels, the Institute of Home Affairs Governance has integrated labour and rural economy issues into its curriculum. Tripartite constituents&#x2019; capacity to foster compliance in the rural economy, particularly within the palm oil and fishing sectors, promote women&#x2019;s empowerment and gender equality, including in the informal economy, has been enhanced through a series of trainings, and workshops. More than 300 people (20 % women) were trained on labour issues in these sectors. Materials and toolkits promoting gender-responsive information, education, and communication concerning labour standards and OSH in rural areas have been developed. Over 1,000 (10 % women) local trade union leaders and members received training on labour rights, with a focus on freedom of association and collective bargaining. Subsequently, 31 enterprise-level collective bargaining agreements were signed, benefiting over 20,000 workers (15 % women). A district-level wage sectoral agreement was signed in West Kalimantan, and more than 15 enterprise-level bipartite Committees (LKS) and OSH committees have been formed. Provincial-level OSH Committees were formed in Riau, East Kalimantan, North Sulawesi, and Maluku. To strengthen Government officials&#x2019; capacity to ensure compliance and promote effective labour inspections, 40 labour inspectors (20 women) have received training. In follow-up to the training, 156 inspection visits were conducted, which identified non-compliance in 15 enterprises and facilitated registration of a total of 10,000 workers with the national security system. The effective functioning of the Palm oil trade union network&#x2019;s joint secretariat has been strengthened. This included the development of a shared case management system, connecting the existing mechanisms of individual federations, and advancing dialogue with the Ministry of Manpower and the Indonesian Palm Oil Association (GAPKI). Throughout 2022 and 2023, the ILO initiated a series of initiatives to address decent work deficits within rural economies, particularly in palm oil and fishing sectors. These efforts included the organization of various training sessions and workshops.
The ILO, in collaboration with the Ministry of Manpower (MoM) as well as employers and workers. developed information, education, and communication (IEC)
materials and toolkits on labour standards and OSH in rural areas, particularly in fishing and palm oil. 
The ILO conducted a series of training sessions for labour inspectors and social partners to promote women workers` rights and prevent and address issues of violence and harassment in the workplace.</narrative>
   </description>
